ct / ctc output exception
=========================

ct (the language) and ctc (its compiler) are free software: you can redistribute
and/or modify ctc under the terms of the GNU General Public License as published
by the Free Software Foundation, either version 3 of the License, or (at your
option) any later version.  The full text of that license is in LICENSE.

As a special exception, when this file or any part of the ctc source is copied
by ctc into a generated output file, you may use that output file without
restriction.  This special exception was added in version 0.0.1 of ctc and
follows the precedent of GNU Bison, whose generated parser files carry the
equivalent clause.

In plain terms, the GNU GPL does NOT apply to:

  * the .ct sources you write and hand to ctc;
  * the .c and .h files ctc generates from them, including the boilerplate ctc
    itself inserts (the vtable structs, the `__<trait>_vptr` fields, the
    `_self` cast line and the vtable variables);
  * the programs you build from that generated code.

You may license your own .ct sources and the generated output however you like,
including under a proprietary license.  The exception covers the generated
output only; the ctc binary and its sources remain under the GNU GPL.


